-- REVIEW INVENTORY
-- Core tables/columns: 12 / 131. Core enum types/labels: 5 / 20.
-- Core indexes including 12 primary-key indexes: 48 total:
--   12 primary keys, 8 unique indexes, 28 ordinary indexes.
-- Core relation foreign keys: 22.
--
-- Full schema reconciliation against the audit:
--   12 core + 21 feature tables = 33 tables.
--   131 core + 214 feature columns = 345 columns.
--   5 core + 9 feature enum types = 14 enums.
--   48 core + 70 feature indexes (49 explicit indexes + 21 PK indexes) = 118.
--   22 core + 54 feature foreign keys = 76.
--
-- The audit's "69 missing indexes" figure subtracts 49 explicit feature
-- CREATE INDEX statements from 118, but does not count the 21 feature-table
-- primary-key indexes created by their CREATE TABLE statements. Thus 69 is
-- not the number of indexes needed in this core migration: the core requires
-- 48 indexes total. The audit's live-schema result remains correct: all 118
-- expected indexes were absent from public at audit time.
--
-- AUTH / RBAC / AUDIT / TENANT REVIEW
-- * SUPER_ADMIN remains representable with nullable User.schoolId.
-- * User, Session and Token uniqueness and account/token state are preserved.
-- * School/User cyclic references are added after all core tables exist.
-- * AuditLog nullable references retain ON DELETE SET NULL.
-- * No RLS, composite tenant foreign keys, or change to schoolIsolation is
--   implied by this schema-faithful SQL. Individual FKs do not themselves
--   prevent references across different schools.
--
-- UNRESOLVED PRODUCT / DESIGN QUESTIONS
-- 1. Class has nullable section/academicYear in its composite unique key.
-- 2. FeeInvoice has nullable structureId in its composite unique key in the
--    existing feature migration.
-- PostgreSQL's normal UNIQUE behavior (NULL values are distinct) is mirrored
-- here because it is Prisma's declared schema behavior. Confirm whether that
-- behavior is intended. If not, update the Prisma model and all affected
-- migration plans only after an explicit product decision and disposable-DB
-- verification. No alternate NULL semantics are guessed in this review.
--
-- OTHER SCHEMA DIFFS TO TRACK
-- The checked-in announcements migration declares Announcement.audienceRoles
-- as NOT NULL, while Prisma 6.12's SQL generated from the current schema
-- declares the array with DEFAULT ARRAY[]::"Role"[] but without NOT NULL.
-- This is an actual nullability mismatch. Do not rewrite that migration.
-- Before adoption, decide and document the intended database invariant, then
-- reconcile it with a reviewed forward migration and/or a supported Prisma
-- schema representation; verify the final diff on the disposable database.
-- If preserving the stricter NOT NULL migration constraint is intentional,
-- establish how Prisma schema drift will be prevented/handled before approval.
--
-- The existing exam migration includes three CHECK constraints not represented
-- in Prisma: ExamSubject_mark_limits_check, ExamMark_absence_marks_check, and
-- ExamGradeBand_percentage_range_check. Keep and test them; they are outside
-- this core SQL. Tenant-consistent cross-table ownership is enforced by
-- application policy, not by the individual Prisma foreign keys.
